I think there is an issue here. Suppose Sigma is ostensibly a
covariance matrix but has a slightly negative numerically-computed
eigenvalue. If it is the case that Sigma is constructed in a way that
guarantees positive definiteness in symbolic math so that eigenvalue
would have been slightly positive if it were computed with more
precision, then a reasonably policy would be "fudge the negative
eigenvalue upward a little bit and continue". But if it is the case
that Sigma is not constructed in a way that guarantees positive
definiteness, then a reasonable policy would be "throw a runtime error
explaining the situation to the user and suggesting the use of one of
stan's built-in ways to sample covariance matrices". However, I don't
see how multi_normal_log() can currently distinguish between these two
cases just by looking at the eigenvalues or the .info() or whatever.

> Given the flexibility noted above, we can't check
> semantic constraints at compile time.

OK. I see Daniel's rationale better now. I haven't pushed yet but I
made some functions in my local tree that update lp and return a
Cholesky factor to go along with (modified) existing functions that
update lp and return Sigma. Actually, now the second are just calling
the first and returning Sigma := L * L.transpose() instead of L. But
we should try to utilize multi_normal_log(y, mu, L) as much as
possible and reserve multi_normal_log(y, mu, Sigma) for the case where
users make their own Sigma somehow (probably without ensuring positive
definiteness).

