I'll check after I get done chuckling at the name,
"vanilla_ice.stan".  Now I'm dealing with the cognitive
dissonance from vanilla_ice being more complex
than ice.

The doc in ice.stan says the results are fairly close
to those from JAGS.  This may be, but they'll probably
be even closer when sigma is declared double(0,1)
rather than double(0,) -- it's sampled sigma ~ uniform(0,1),
so you need the constraint.

Also, I'm not sure if normal(0,1000)
was intended -- we're using deviation, not
variance, as the parameter, so this is pretty
flat!  But then BUGS uses 1E-6, so this is
probably what you meant.

And why that crazy sigma * 1E3 scale param for
beta[1] and beta[2].  Does it really relate to
sigma?  Why not just draw beta[1] and beta[2]
from something relatively flat?  Is it that we
expect sigma to be close to 1 so this actually
does something to the inferences?

vanilla_ice.stan
----------------

This model's really confusing (like Wawro's!).

I don't see a sigma in your model at all.  Is
it still going to be 1/sqrt(tau)?  You can define
a "generated quantities" block to generate things
like this (more efficient than adding a derived parameter,
which gets calculated every leapfrog step within HMC)_.

And while I see tau getting sampled from a gamma(shape,d),
I don't see it touching any other data.  It's used to
define betasd[k], but then betasd[k] isn't used
anywhere else.  So I'd expect tau to just be
distributed as gamma(shape,d) in the posterior.

I doubt that's what you intended, because in
the BUGS model, tau is also used in defining
betaprec[k], which is then used as the precision
parameter for sampling beta[k].

- Bob

