OK, I've confused myself here and quite possibly other people too. So
let me see if I have this correct now.

According to the BUGSExamples R package, this model

https://r-forge.r-project.org/scm/viewvc.php/pkg/inst/examples/Icemodel.txt?view=markup&revision=2&root=bugs-examples

can be estimated in WinBUGS and OpenBUGS but not JAGS. That is
apparently due to the fact that it has what JAGS calls a "cycle".
However, this "cycle" is not actually a cycle in the sense of graph
theory because the graph at the bottom of

www.mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk/bugs/documentation/exampVol2/node13.html <http://www.mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk/bugs/documentation/exampVol2/node13.html>

is acyclic in the sense that you cannot get back to where you started
from anywhere in the mess in the top right corner. It is only
classified as a "cycle" by JAGS because the parameters are all
contained in the same vector named beta and JAGS does not distinguish
among elements of beta.

So, Stan should be able to do that model, correct?
But, Stan cannot do models with an undirected graph? Like

www.mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk/bugs/documentation/exampVol2/node14.html#iceSigfig <http://www.mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk/bugs/documentation/exampVol2/node14.html#iceSigfig>

If so, the question is "Why is vanilla_ice yielding estimates that are
slightly different from those in WinBUGS?" rather than "Why is
pineapple_ice yielding estimates that are slightly different from
those WinBUGS gets when estimating the vanilla_ice model?" Although
the question of why pineapple_ice hangs during burnin if sigma is not
constrained to be sufficiently positive is worth investigating (unless
the answer is simply that Stan doesn't do models that correspond to
undirected graphs).

Ben

