Just to be clear:  the example below _wouldn't_ actually hack an MI model.  Rather, it's a "pseudo-likelihood" (which I think might be the same as "naive Bayes") model in which, instead of working with p(x,y), you work with p(x|y)*p(y|x), for computational convenience.  We might be able to come up with a setting where it would make sense, but MI isn't it.
A

