>> I don't see how to maintain these states given
>> the flexibility of Eigen matrice's operator[],
>> which returns a REFERNENCE to an element:
>>
>>      Matrix<T,...>
>>
>>      T&  operator[](size_t n) { ... }
>>
>> If someone gets such a reference and changes it,
>> we can't guarantee any of our checks like isInverted
>> or isPosDef or whatever still hold.
>
> In the CovMatrix class in my head, there would be constructors that
> would take parameters and output a CovMatrix with the various flags
> set. But I can't think of a great reason why some rogue function
> should subsequently be changing its elements before the posterior is
> evaluated. I might even overload [] and () to throw errors.

Are you suggesting we extend Eigen with a CovMatrix class?

The nice thing about this is that we could have constructors
that only took the lower-triangular portion of the CovMatrix
efficiently.  That'd save half the auto-dif variables compared
to how it's being done now (which could certainly be improved
to at least exploit the self-adjoint views, but that gets back
to bad interactions with heterogeneous operators in auto-dif
in Eigen).

It shouldn't be that hard to extend the Stan language
to treat cov-matrix as a different type than matrix and
declare the multi_normal densities to require a covar matrix
(or precision matrix).  Right now, you can plug any matrix
in and the function checks the arguments are OK if error-handling
is turned on.

 > But if
> there were some reason why a function should be changing its elements,
> then I would say it is that function's responsibility to set CheckMe
> to true so that downstream functions know to go through all the
> checks.

This is the kind of thing I don't see at all how to do inside
the Stan modeling language.  But then we might not need to.

We could have the multivariate densities call a
a checkMe() method on a covariance matrix before it's used.
Then we keep a cached answer that's cleared whenever something
changes the matrix in an unsound way.

We can handle caching of Cholesky factors, log determinants,
etc., the same way.

> Maybe I haven't emphasized the following enough:
>
> -- There are a small number of ways to construct a covariance matrix
> that guarantee it will be positive definite

I get that.  It's why we're testing, so we can let
the user know something's going wrong.

I also see the benefit of the kind of positive-definiteness
safety you're talking about.

In fact, there are lots of other places we can use it,
like transforming parameters.  We don't want someone doing:

   parameter { double y; }
   model { log(y) ~ normal(0,1); }

thinking they're going to get a log normal.  They can do this
and then add the (in this case very simple) log Jacobian determinant,
but if they just write the above, it's not going to do what
they might expect.

Another big potential source of error is not
declaring parameters with appropriate transforms.
The degenerate case of covariance matrixes is
the univariate case.  There, you need a positive
scale parameter.  There's nothing to prevent the
user from writing:

   y ~ normal(0,-1);

Now we could write a positive-number class, that only
allowed positive number operations on it (square,
square root, log if the value's greater than 1, multiplying
by another positive number).  Then require scale parameters
to belong to this class.

Maybe I've been inspired by C++ itself, but I've
given users a nice length rope with which to hang
themselves.

> -- And there are a great many ways of constructing a covariance matrix
> that do not have a snowball's chance in hell of being positive
> definite in all 1000 (or even 3) iterations. The subset of positive
> definite matrices within the set of symmetric matrices with positive
> diagonal elements is just too small.

Right.  This is going to be a problem if someone
doesn't declare a matrix parameter as a covar_matrix.

If they initialize to a covariance matrix (we don't
like the snowball's chances on random init), then very
small movements should maintain positive definiteness,
but this is definitely not something we should encourage.

> So, although we probably can't absolutely prevent the following from
> happening:
>
> parameters {
>    double covariances[K * (K - 1) / 2 + K];
> }
> transformed parameters {
>    double Sigma[K,K];
>    int count;
>
>    count<- 1;
>    for (i in 1:K) {
>      for (j in i:K) {
>        Sigma[i,j]<- covariances[count];
>        Sigma[j,i]<- Sigma[i,j]
>        count<- count + 1;
>      }
> }
>
> We need to do everything we can to discourage the user from doing that
> because Sigma will not be positive definite in the vast majority of
> the iterations. Users should be using convenience functions in
> their .stan files like makeLKJ(), makeAR1(), etc. that output valid
> covariance matrices. And when those functions are used, we need some
> way of indicating that the density should not check Sigma.

The main method of discouragement at this point is that the
programs won't work.

Which reminds me that we need to instrument them to somehow
stop and fail under some conditions.

> The downside is that maybe in the future somebody invents some cool
> way of constructing a positive definite covariance matrix that a user
> wants to use but isn't implemented in Stan. In that case, they would
> have to do it themselves within the .stan file. But I would almost
> rather them email a feature request.

We can always do that.  Either the way we
have with the LKJ business, or by adding new
density functions.

I hope to make Stan extensible soon so that
users can define their own functions and add
them to the symbol table so they play nicely with
all the (basic) type checking.

- Bob

